error": ""}, {"step_id": "step_16", "tool": "compute", "args": {"expression": "inter/(a+b-inter)", "values": {"inter": 26, "a": 40, "b": 36}}, "result_digest": "8f7e8cfc65e7972f", "result_summary": "{\"expression\": \"inter/(a+b-inter)\", \"result\": 0.52}", "latency_ms": 0, "tokens_before": 56669, "tokens_after": 56669, "ok": true, "error": ""}], "final_claim": {"task_id": "t_459f9d4b", "decision": "absent", "mechanism": "regime_switch", "entities": [103, 462, 719, 836, 866, 1224, 1318, 1374, 1415, 1598, 1610, 1826], "window": [25166, 55166], "confidence": 0.76, "evidence": [{"trace_id": "step_01", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_16", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_03", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_09", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_10", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_13", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_02", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_11", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_04", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_14", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_12", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "No abrupt regime switch. Turnover stays flat (0.23-0.44) with the peak at t=35294 only 0.444, no spike. Before/after contact sets at that point overlap at Jaccard 0.52, sharing 26 partners, so the old contact set is retained rather than replaced. The edge-level churn is explained by group members rotating through sequential non-overlapping phases (719 in 27666-32665, 1224 in 35166-40165, 1598 in 47666-52665) while contacting the same persistent partner pool; the pool nodes themselves are continuously active. No activity bursts and uniform per-member footprints rule out volume and popularity explanations, and the Jaccard=0.0 in the far-window diff reflects member-keyed edge pairs, not new partners."}, "raw_final_response": "## Analysis\n\n**Turnover is flat, not abrupt (step_01).** The change series over the full period stays in a narrow band (0.23\u20130.44) with the \"peak\" at t=35294 reaching only 0.4444 \u2014 barely above the 0.32\u20130.43 baseline. A genuine regime switch produces a spike near 1.0 against a low floor; here there is no such spike.\n\n**The contact pool itself persists across the candidate change point (step_01, step_16).** Before-peak contacts (40 nodes) and after-peak contacts (36 nodes) share 26 members \u2014 57, 156, 234, 235, 255, 324, 354, 402, 535, 559, 617, 641, 728, 756, 874, 931, 1010, 1133, 1141, 1182, 1312, 1357, 1416, 1422, 1436, 1538 \u2014 a Jaccard overlap of ~0.52. The old set does **not** give way to a new set; it is largely retained.\n\n**What actually changes is which member speaks, not who is spoken to.** The edge-level diff (step_03) shows the 79 \"removed\" and 36 \"added\" edges are the *same partner pool* re-attached to a different group member: `719\u2192{57,156,234,...}` becomes `1224\u2192{57,156,234,...}`. Members occupy sequential, non-overlapping phases: 719 is active only in 27666\u201332665 (step_09), 1224 only in 35166\u201340165 (step_10), 1598 only in 47666\u201352665 (step_13). This rotation also explains the misleading Jaccard=0.0 in step_12 \u2014 that metric keys on (member, partner) pairs, so a member handoff registers as total edge replacement even though partner identities are unchanged.\n\n**Confounders ruled out:**\n- *Volume / burst artifact*: no bins exceed z=1.5 (step_11); unique-edge counts vary smoothly 33\u201366 with no discontinuity at 35294 (step_02).\n- *Popularity or activity asymmetry among members*: all 12 members have near-identical footprints (92\u201397 events, 31\u201336 partners; step_04), so no member's arrival injects a genuinely new neighbourhood.\n- *Partner pool churn*: pool nodes 57, 234, 535, 1436 are each active ~39\u201340 events across the full period (step_14), i.e. continuously present rather than appearing/disappearing at a change point. The pairwise absences (steps 05\u201308) are consequences of member phase-gating, not partner replacement.\n\n**Conclusion:** the apparent change point is an artifact of members rotating over a stable, shared partner pool \u2014 gradual/structural handoff, not an abrupt old-set\u2192new-set transition.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_459f9d4b\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [103, 462, 719, 836, 866, 1224, 1318, 1374, 1415, 1598, 1610, 1826],\n  \"window\": [25166, 55166],\n  \"confidence\": 0.76,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_16\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"No abrupt regime switch. Turnover stays flat (0.23-0.44) with the peak at t=35294 only 0.444, no spike. Before/after contact sets at that point overlap at Jaccard 0.52, sharing 26 partners, so the old contact set is retained rather than replaced. The edge-level churn is explained by group members rotating through sequential non-overlapping phases (719 in 27666-32665, 1224 in 35166-40165, 1598 in 47666-52665) while contacting the same persistent partner pool; the pool nodes themselves are continuously active. No activity bursts and uniform per-member footprints rule out volume and popularity explanations, and the Jaccard=0.0 in the far-window diff reflects member-keyed edge pairs, not new partners.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 68078, "total_output_tokens": 6760, "total_tokens": 74838, "total_latency_ms": 99712, "llm_calls": 8, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}